      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "© 2013, Wiley Periodicals, Inc.",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
            "Label": "OBJECTIVES",
            "content": "The aim of this study was to examine the angiographic result and its outcome predictors using the combination of paclitaxel-eluting balloon (PEB) and Genous stent."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
            "Label": "BACKGROUND",
            "content": "This approach to treat coronary stenoses is a logical strategy to strike a balance between minimizing restenosis and stent thrombosis."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "METHODS",
            "content": "From November 2010 to June 2012, 40 symptomatic patients with 44 de novo coronary lesions of diameter stenosis ≥ 50% were treated with the combination of PEB and Genous stents. Angiographic and clinical follow-up were intended at 6 and 9 months, respectively."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
            "Label": "RESULTS",
            "content": "The mean age of patients was 61 ± 11 years, with male predominance (83%). Diabetes mellitus and end-stage renal failure on peritoneal dialysis were found in 15 (38%) and 10 (25%) patients, respectively. Patients received dual antiplatelet therapy for 5.1 ± 1.5 months post procedure. The size and length of PEB used was larger than the stents (3.13 ± 0.46 mm and 28 ± 9 mm vs. 2.98 ± 0.36 mm and 23 ± 7 mm). Restudy angiography was performed on 41 (95%) lesions in 37 (93%) patients at 5.9 ± 1.7 months. Angiographic restenosis was seen in 5 (12%) lesions, and significantly associated with diabetes mellitus and dialysis dependency; the late lumen loss was 0.38 ± 0.37 mm. At 9-month follow-up, no stent thrombosis was observed."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "content": "The use of PEB combined with Genous stent is associated with a reasonably low restenosis and late lumen loss, whereas diabetes mellitus and renal failure with dialysis are poor predictors of angiographic restenosis."
          }
        ]
      },
